---
/**
 * 02 — Technical Worlds. The site's one large motion moment, and the one place
 * the paper ground inverts to ink (docs/MOTION_SYSTEM.md §3).
 *
 * This wrapper is deliberately thin. It exists to do four things that an
 * island cannot do for itself:
 *
 * 1. **Receive `t` as a prop.** Components never reach for a dictionary
 *    (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md §3, rule 1); the page resolves the locale once and
 *    passes the strings down. The island therefore has no locale logic at all.
 *
 * 2. **Place the band in the numbered sequence.** `ui/Section.astro` derives
 *    the element `id`, the figure number and the anchor from one checked prop:
 *    `section="worlds"` is a member of a union over `SECTION_IDS`, so the `02`
 *    is `SECTION_IDS.indexOf('worlds')` rather than a literal that reordering
 *    the array could silently strand. The margin word comes from
 *    `t.sections.worlds`, authored sentence-case and uppercased in CSS.
 *
 *    This replaced a hand-rolled `02` eyebrow inside the island. That literal
 *    was correct on the day it was written, which is exactly what made it a
 *    liability — and it also meant the band was the one section on the page
 *    with no localized margin word and no `id` to link to.
 *
 * 3. **Choose the hydration directive.** `client:media` is load-bearing, not a
 *    default. React is the only framework runtime on this site and it must
 *    never touch the critical rendering path (ARCHITECTURE §5).
 *
 *    This band previously hydrated on `client:visible`, which was correct
 *    about *when* and silent about *whether*. Every visitor who scrolled this
 *    far downloaded React, the renderer and the island — measured 67.2 KB gz —
 *    to hydrate a component whose own first act was to check `matchMedia` and
 *    decide not to build the timeline. A phone paid the whole runtime to
 *    re-render, identically, markup it had already been sent as HTML.
 *
 *    `HYDRATION_QUERY` is `useMediaQuery.ts`'s own traverse breakpoint plus a
 *    motion clause, imported rather than restated so the download gate and the
 *    timeline gate cannot drift apart. The island keeps both of its runtime
 *    checks: this decides what is fetched, those decide what is built, and
 *    neither trusts the other. GSAP stays behind a dynamic import inside the
 *    island, so it is fetched later still and only if the traverse runs.
 *
 *    Mobile, short-window and reduced-motion visitors now download **nothing**
 *    for this section. What they see is the server-rendered stack, which is
 *    the same composition they were already being shown.
 *
 *    The cost, stated plainly: with no island on the mobile path there is also
 *    no `IntersectionObserver` marking one panel at a time as active, so the
 *    five stages are static there rather than ambient. That is the trade
 *    docs/REDESIGN_DECISIONS.md #11 accepts — 60 KB of framework was not a
 *    fair price for a breathing SVG, and MOTION_SYSTEM §7 already prefers
 *    static over janky on a phone. If it is ever wanted back, it is a CSS
 *    scroll-driven `animation-timeline: view()` at zero bytes of JavaScript,
 *    which is the same mechanism the layer spine already uses.
 *
 * 4. **Own the band's stylesheet.** The styles are `is:global` because the
 *    markup they target is rendered by React, which Astro's scoping attribute
 *    does not reach. Every selector is namespaced under `.tw`/`.tw-`, and every
 *    colour is a `var(--color-*)` from `src/styles/tokens.css` — there is not
 *    one raw colour value below, and not one inline `style` attribute in the
 *    markup (see the CSP note in the island).
 *
 * ## Where `data-stops-hero-visual` sits, and why it is not on the `<section>`
 *
 * The hero's canvas stops while this band is on screen, and the marker for that
 * is `data-stops-hero-visual`. It belongs on `<section id="worlds">`, but
 * `ui/Section.astro` renders a fixed attribute list and does not spread
 * undeclared props — verified against a production build, where the attribute
 * was accepted at the call site and silently absent from the output. Rather
 * than ship a contract that looks right in source and does nothing live, it is
 * rendered by the island onto `.tw-band`, the section's only child.
 *
 * An `IntersectionObserver` on that element reports the same thing: it fills
 * the section minus its padding, so the band is detected a few rem later on
 * approach, which any rootMargin absorbs. Moving it onto the `<section>` needs
 * one line in `ui/Section.astro` (a rest spread onto the element); that file
 * has another owner.
 *
 * ## What gets pinned
 *
 * `Section` renders the outer `<section id="worlds">`, and `class="tw on-ink"`
 * puts the band's own styling and the ink inversion on it. That element — not
 * the island's root — is what ScrollTrigger pins, so the `02 / TECHNICAL
 * WORLDS` annotation stays on screen with the traverse rather than scrolling
 * away above it. The island finds it with `closest('.tw')`; the contract
 * between these two files is that one `.tw` class.
 *
 * The island itself is server-rendered by this file at build time, so all five
 * domains are complete, semantic HTML in document order before any JavaScript
 * exists. With scripting off, the section is a readable stack of five panels.
 */

import Section from '../ui/Section.astro';
import type { Locale, UIStrings } from '../../i18n/types';
import TechnicalWorlds from '../visuals/worlds/TechnicalWorlds';
import { HYDRATION_QUERY } from '../visuals/worlds/useMediaQuery';

interface Props {
  /** Resolved dictionary for the current locale. */
  t: UIStrings;
  /**
   * Current locale. Part of the shared home-section prop contract; this band
   * renders no locale-dependent URL, so it is not forwarded to the island.
   */
  locale: Locale;
  /**
   * Base path. Same contract, same reason: Technical Worlds links nowhere, so
   * nothing here concatenates a URL. If that changes, route it through
   * `src/i18n/routing.ts` rather than building a path by hand.
   */
  base: string;
}

const { t } = Astro.props;

/** Checked against `SECTION_IDS`; supplies the id, the figure number and the word. */
const SECTION = 'worlds' as const;
---

<Section
  section={SECTION}
  label={t.sections[SECTION]}
  labelledBy="tw-heading"
  width="wide"
  spacing="lg"
  class="tw on-ink"
>
  <TechnicalWorlds client:media={HYDRATION_QUERY} t={t} />
</Section>

<style is:global>
  /* ===========================================================================
     TECHNICAL WORLDS — the ink band
     Global by necessity (the markup is React's), namespaced by discipline.
     No raw colour values. All motion is CSS animation over static geometry;
     the only scripted animation in the section is the GSAP traverse itself.
     ======================================================================== */

  /* --- Domain accents -----------------------------------------------------
     `--tw-accent` is the one thing that differs between the five stages, and
     it is resolved here rather than by an inline `style` on each panel.

     That is a CSP requirement, not a preference. `astro.config.mjs` enables
     `security.csp` with no `'unsafe-inline'`, and Astro emits no
     `style-src-attr`, so `style-src` governs style *attributes* too — with no
     `'unsafe-hashes'`, every inline `style=""` on the page is dropped. An
     island that painted its accents that way would render five identical grey
     stages in production and look perfect in `astro dev`.

     `data-domain` is on the panel, the stage and the rail stop, so one rule per
     domain covers all three and the value inherits to every descendant.

     These five lines mirror `Domain['accentVar']`, which is typed
     `--color-domain-${DomainId}`. CSS cannot derive the name, so a sixth domain
     needs a sixth line here — `tests/worlds.test.ts` fails if one is missing.

     Ink ground only, per the warning in tokens.css: the band is `.on-ink`, and
     these hues measure 1.72:1–2.77:1 on paper.
     --------------------------------------------------------------------- */

  .tw [data-domain='ai'] {
    --tw-accent: var(--color-domain-ai);
  }
  .tw [data-domain='quantum'] {
    --tw-accent: var(--color-domain-quantum);
  }
  .tw [data-domain='fpga'] {
    --tw-accent: var(--color-domain-fpga);
  }
  .tw [data-domain='electronics'] {
    --tw-accent: var(--color-domain-electronics);
  }
  .tw [data-domain='audio'] {
    --tw-accent: var(--color-domain-audio);
  }

  /* The measure, the vertical rhythm and the `02 / TECHNICAL WORLDS` margin
     annotation all come from `ui/Section.astro` and `ui/Container.astro`, which
     wrap this island. Only what those two do not know about lives here.

     `spacing="lg"` supplies the stacked padding — the token comment reserves
     `--spacing-section-lg` for the hero and this band exactly. */
  .tw {
    position: relative;

    /* The pitch of the traverse: how much of the viewport one domain panel
       occupies while pinned, and therefore how far the track advances per
       stop. The remaining 14% is the peek of the next domain, which is what
       tells the reader the band continues sideways.

       It is a custom property because three rules have to agree on it — the
       panel's flex basis, the track's two spacers and the viewport's edge
       mask — and the first two were silently allowed to disagree before. See
       the spacer rule below. */
    --tw-panel-pitch: 86%;

    /* Half the peek, which is also the distance from the frame's edge to the
       centred panel's own edge. Everything that has to stay clear of the
       active panel is expressed against this rather than against a literal. */
    --tw-peek: calc((100% - var(--tw-panel-pitch)) / 2);
  }

  /* The pinned composition. `data-traverse` is set imperatively by the island
     *after* it has confirmed a wide viewport, motion is allowed and GSAP has
     actually loaded — so a failed import leaves the readable stack rather than
     a broken slider.

     The extra `.section` in each selector is not decoration. Astro compiles its
     scoped rules to `.section--lg[data-astro-cid-…]`, which is (0,2,0) — the
     same specificity as `.tw[data-traverse='true']`, so a plain override would
     be decided by stylesheet order. `.tw.section[…]` is (0,3,0) and wins
     outright. */
  .tw.section[data-traverse='true'] {
    height: 100svh;
    padding-block: var(--spacing-block);
  }

  /* A pinned full-height section needs every wrapper between it and the track
     to stretch, or the viewport row collapses to its content height. */
  .tw.section[data-traverse='true'] .section__inner {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-rows: auto minmax(0, 1fr);
    height: 100%;
  }

  .tw.section[data-traverse='true'] .section__figure {
    margin-bottom: var(--spacing-gutter);
  }

  .tw-band {
    min-height: 0;
  }

  .tw[data-traverse='true'] .tw-band {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-rows: auto minmax(0, 1fr) auto;
    row-gap: var(--spacing-stack);
    height: 100%;
  }

  .tw-header__subtitle {
    max-width: var(--container-prose);
    margin-top: var(--spacing-gutter);
    color: var(--color-on-ink-muted);
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  .tw[data-traverse='true'] .tw-header__subtitle {
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
  }

  /* --- Viewport and track -------------------------------------------------
     Stacked by default. That is the server-rendered state, the no-JavaScript
     state, the reduced-motion state and the mobile state — one composition
     serving four cases rather than four special paths.
     --------------------------------------------------------------------- */

  .tw-viewport {
    position: relative;
  }

  .tw-track {
    display: grid;
    row-gap: var(--spacing-section);
  }

  /* The frame clips at 100% of its width, and for most of the traverse a
     neighbouring `h3` or `p` is crossing that boundary — measured on the
     deployed build, some text fragment was hard-cut for 83% of the pinned
     scroll, chopped mid-word ("Computac", "clási") with nothing to say it was
     a frame edge rather than the end of the sentence.

     The gradient mask makes the boundary a fade instead of a guillotine. Its
     width is derived from `--tw-peek`, so it can never reach the centred
     panel's own text: the active panel starts one full peek in from each edge,
     and the fade is at most 68% of that. The `min()` caps it on very wide
     windows, where a peek is large enough that a proportional fade would start
     washing out the neighbour's whole approach rather than just its edge.

     `black`/`transparent` are alpha, not colour — the mask carries no hue and
     names none, so tokens.css remains the only place a colour is chosen. Both
     spellings are declared: without the prefixed one WebKit renders no mask at
     all, which is the defect back for a large share of the traffic. */
  .tw[data-traverse='true'] .tw-viewport {
    --tw-fade: min(64px, calc(var(--tw-peek) * 0.68));

    height: 100%;
    overflow: hidden;
    -webkit-mask-image: linear-gradient(
      to right,
      transparent 0,
      black var(--tw-fade),
      black calc(100% - var(--tw-fade)),
      transparent 100%
    );
    mask-image: linear-gradient(
      to right,
      transparent 0,
      black var(--tw-fade),
      black calc(100% - var(--tw-fade)),
      transparent 100%
    );
  }

  .tw[data-traverse='true'] .tw-track {
    display: flex;
    align-items: stretch;
    height: 100%;
    row-gap: 0;
    will-change: transform;
  }

  /* --- The spacers, and the two bugs they fix ---------------------------
     These two rules are what make the traverse's geometry come out whole, and
     the arithmetic is worth stating because both defects were pure layout
     rather than anything visual.

     **Whole-pitch registration** (docs/REDESIGN_DECISIONS.md #15). Five panels
     at 86% make a track 430% of the viewport wide, so a bare track travels
     430% − 100% = 330%. The traverse has four steps between five stops, so
     each step moves 330 / 4 = 82.5% — while the panels are pitched 86% apart.
     The 3.5% shortfall accumulates: at stop *i* the outgoing panel is left
     standing 3.5×*i* percent inside the frame. Measured at 1440×900, viewport
     box 1344px: slivers of 46, 102, 157 and 213px at the four stops after the
     first, each showing the clipped tail of the previous stage's annotation.
     They were not a transient — they were where the traverse came to rest.

     Adding 14% of spacer fixes that, because it makes the travel a whole
     number of pitches:

       track = 14% + 5 × 86% = 444%   travel = 444% − 100% = 344% = 4 × 86%

     **Centring.** That 14% used to be one trailing spacer, and *where* it sat
     decided where the panels sat. With all of it after the last panel, panel
     0's centre was half a peek — 94px at 1440×900 — right of the frame's
     centre at zero translation, and it only moved further away from there. The
     first world therefore never reached the middle of the frame at all
     (measured closest approach: 94.6px), and the last one passed through the
     centre and settled 94px past it. Neither is a taste question: the pinned
     composition's whole claim is that one domain is being *shown*.

     Splitting the same 14% into a leading and a trailing half moves every
     panel half a peek left and makes the arithmetic exact — stop *i* is
     centred at a translation of exactly −*i* × pitch, and the last stop's
     translation is exactly the full travel. Neither the track width nor the
     travel distance changes by a pixel; the island's `distance()` and `pitch()`
     pick it up for free, because they measure `scrollWidth`.

     Pseudo-elements rather than real ones: they are spacers, they carry no
     content, and they must not exist in the stacked composition, where the
     track is a grid and empty children would add rows and row-gaps. Declaring
     them only here means they are never generated there. */
  .tw[data-traverse='true'] .tw-track::before,
  .tw[data-traverse='true'] .tw-track::after {
    content: '';
    flex: 0 0 var(--tw-peek);
  }

  .tw-viewport:focus-visible {
    outline: var(--border-rule) solid var(--focus-ring);
    outline-offset: 6px;
  }

  /* --- Panel --------------------------------------------------------------
     Each domain is one channel of the instrument, so it carries its accent on
     a left rule — the `--border-heavy` case the token file reserves for
     exactly this. During the traverse the rule thins to a hairline and only
     the domain in the viewport is emphatic, which is position feedback that
     costs no extra element and hides no text.
     --------------------------------------------------------------------- */

  .tw-panel {
    display: grid;
    align-content: center;
    row-gap: var(--spacing-stack);
    padding-left: var(--spacing-gutter);
    border-left: var(--border-heavy) solid var(--tw-accent);
  }

  .tw[data-traverse='true'] .tw-panel {
    flex: 0 0 var(--tw-panel-pitch);
    min-width: 0;
    padding-right: var(--spacing-block);
    border-left-width: var(--border-hairline);
    border-left-color: var(--color-ink-rule);
    transition:
      border-left-color var(--duration-base) var(--ease-standard),
      border-left-width var(--duration-base) var(--ease-standard);
  }

  .tw[data-traverse='true'] .tw-panel[data-current='true'] {
    border-left-width: var(--border-heavy);
    border-left-color: var(--tw-accent);
  }

  .tw-panel__meta {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-wide);
  }

  .tw-panel__layer {
    color: var(--tw-accent);
  }

  .tw-panel__name {
    margin-top: 0.5rem;
  }

  .tw-panel__summary {
    max-width: 38ch;
    margin-top: var(--spacing-gutter);
    color: var(--color-on-ink-muted);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  .tw-panel__stage {
    min-width: 0;
    max-width: 34rem;
  }

  @media (min-width: 48rem) {
    .tw-panel {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 24rem) minmax(0, 1fr);
      column-gap: var(--spacing-block);
      align-items: center;
      row-gap: 0;
    }
  }

  /* --- Footer: position rail and keyboard hint --------------------------- */

  .tw-footer {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--spacing-gutter);
    margin-top: var(--spacing-block);
  }

  .tw[data-traverse='true'] .tw-footer {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  .tw-rail {
    display: none;
    gap: 6px;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .tw[data-traverse='true'] .tw-rail {
    display: flex;
  }

  .tw-rail__stop {
    width: 30px;
    height: var(--border-rule);
    background-color: var(--color-ink-rule);
    transition:
      background-color var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-standard),
      width var(--duration-base) var(--ease-standard);
  }

  .tw-rail__stop[data-current='true'] {
    width: 60px;
    background-color: var(--tw-accent);
  }

  /* Screen-reader available at all times, and visible while the traverse has
     keyboard focus — so a sighted keyboard user is told what the arrow keys
     do at the moment the arrow keys start working. */
  .tw-hint {
    position: absolute;
    width: 1px;
    height: 1px;
    margin: -1px;
    overflow: hidden;
    clip-path: inset(50%);
    white-space: nowrap;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-caption);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-label);
    color: var(--color-on-ink-muted);
  }

  .tw-band:has(.tw-viewport:focus-visible) .tw-hint {
    position: static;
    width: auto;
    height: auto;
    margin: 0;
    overflow: visible;
    clip-path: none;
    white-space: normal;
  }

  /* ===========================================================================
     VISUAL STAGES

     One frame, one coordinate space, one annotation voice, five behaviours.
     `--tw-accent` is set per panel from the domain's `accentVar`, so a stage
     never names a colour.

     Every animation below is declared ONLY under `[data-active='true']`. An
     inactive stage has no animation to pause — it simply has none. And no
     animated element has a hidden resting state: with the animation absent,
     each one resolves to its visible form (audit 2.3 / MOTION_SYSTEM §6).
     ======================================================================== */

  .tw-stage {
    position: relative;

    /* The activation grammar's clock, declared once. `--duration-deliberate`
       is 720ms, the token MOTION_SYSTEM §3's 400–1000ms arrival was specified
       against; `--tw-arrive-lag` is the beat the incoming world waits before
       it claims the accent; `--tw-settle` is when ambient is allowed to start.
       See the ACTIVATION GRAMMAR block below. */
    --tw-arrive: var(--duration-deliberate);
    --tw-arrive-lag: 240ms;
    /* Ambient must be *visibly running* by the time the arrival finishes, not
       starting after it. The arrival ends at `--tw-arrive-lag + --tw-arrive` =
       960ms; at the old 800ms the first ambient cycle only became meaningful
       3–7.5s in, which was routinely longer than a world stayed on screen, so
       the settled state was a thing the reader was told about and never shown.

       400ms overlaps the second half of the arrival and is still a whole
       number of FPGA clock ticks (0.4s), which is what keeps the fabric's
       register blink in phase with its own clock. The ambient animations that
       do not have to phase-lock to anything skip this and start mid-cycle
       instead — see the negative delays below. */
    --tw-settle: 400ms;

    /* The local progress channel's resting value. `StageFrame` overwrites this
       through CSSOM while the traverse is running: 0 as the world begins
       arriving, exactly 0.5 while it is centred, 1 once it has left. Declared
       here so a stage can read `var(--tw-progress)` in the stacked
       composition, where nothing is publishing, without a fallback of its
       own. */
    --tw-progress: 0.5;

    /* The electronics chain's clock, and the phase it starts at. Its pulse and
       its five block glyphs are two readings of one event, so they are driven
       off one period with one shared offset — change either here and the
       causal link between them survives. The offset is negative for the same
       reason the ripple's is: the signal is already in flight when the world
       arrives, instead of the reader watching an empty trace until it sets
       off. */
    --tw-chain: 2.4s;
    --tw-chain-phase: -0.9s;
  }

  .tw-stage__meta {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.625rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.625rem;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-stretch: normal;
    font-size: var(--text-micro);
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-wide);
    color: var(--color-on-ink-muted);
  }

  .tw-stage__channel {
    display: block;
    width: 28px;
    height: var(--border-heavy);
    background-color: var(--tw-accent);
  }

  .tw-stage__svg {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    overflow: visible;
  }

  /* Shared drawing vocabulary. */
  .tw-stage__svg .tw-rule {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--color-ink-rule);
    stroke-width: 1;
    vector-effect: non-scaling-stroke;
  }

  .tw-stage__frame .tw-tick {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--tw-accent);
    stroke-width: 1.5;
    vector-effect: non-scaling-stroke;
  }

  .tw-annot {
    fill: var(--color-on-ink-muted);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 8px;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
  }

  /* ===========================================================================
     ACTIVATION GRAMMAR

     Nothing on this band used to *arrive*. Every stage animation was `infinite`
     and opacity-based, declared under `[data-active='true']`, so the
     inactive → active transition was literally "breathing starts". Measured
     over 800ms, three of the five worlds changed 0.005–0.046% of their pixels
     after a 2515px pinned commitment. MOTION_SYSTEM §3 specifies a 400–1000ms
     arrival; it was never built. This is it.

     One grammar, five verbs — same flag, same tokens, a different primitive per
     domain, because the primitive is the thing each domain is *about*:

       ai           the forecast fan opens from the `now` rule
       quantum      the sphere draws itself, then the state vector arrives
       fpga         the selected route draws, its cells latching as it resolves
       electronics  the trace energises left to right through the chain
       audio        the waveform sweeps in, the spectrum rises to meet it

     Four rules hold it together.

     1. **CSS only.** ScrollTrigger owns scroll *position*; it does not own
        state transitions, and a second runtime for one of them would
        contradict MOTION_SYSTEM §5's ownership table. A rule that matches only
        under `[data-active='true']` restarts its animation every time the flag
        flips — which is exactly a one-shot arrival, for 0 KB of JavaScript.

     2. **Ambient waits, then stays perceptible.** Every ambient animation is
        delayed past `--tw-settle`, so a world plays its verb and *then*
        settles. Calmer than the arrival, deliberately not silent: the target
        is the Quantum world's measured 0.41%, not Electronics' 0.005%.

     3. **The outgoing world drops its accent first.** Leaving is
        `--duration-fast` with no delay; arriving is `--duration-base` after
        `--tw-arrive-lag`. For that beat there is no accent anywhere on the
        band, so the change reads as one instrument switching modes rather
        than as two panels cross-fading.

     4. **Hidden state lives inside the keyframes, never at rest.** The audit
        found a resting `opacity: 0` here once, which hid content from exactly
        the readers a reduced-motion rule exists to help. Every `from` below is
        a state no element is in when no animation runs, so `animation: none`
        resolves to the finished picture rather than to nothing — which is what
        makes the reduced-motion block at the foot of this file safe.
     ======================================================================== */

  /* Accent handover. Only while the traverse is engaged: in the stacked
     composition every panel is its own subject and none of them is "outgoing",
     so nothing is dimmed there. */
  .tw-stage__channel,
  .tw-stage__frame .tw-tick {
    opacity: 1;
    transition: opacity var(--duration-base) var(--ease-entrance) var(--tw-arrive-lag);
  }

  .tw[data-traverse='true'] .tw-stage:not([data-active='true']) .tw-stage__channel,
  .tw[data-traverse='true'] .tw-stage:not([data-active='true']) .tw-stage__frame .tw-tick {
    opacity: 0.26;
    transition: opacity var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-exit);
  }

  /* ai — the partition resolves.

     `tw-draw` over a `pathLength="100"` boundary, so the three subpaths draw
     out of nothing in sequence: the model deciding where the classes divide.
     The confidence corridor settles in behind it, and the misclassification
     flags come up last — a flag means nothing until there is a boundary for
     its point to be on the wrong side of.

     The flags' arrival is a `transform`, never an opacity. Their opacity is a
     presentation attribute written on every scroll tick, and a CSS animation
     over it would win permanently and freeze all nine. */
  .tw-stage[data-active='true'] .tw-dl-boundary {
    animation: tw-draw var(--tw-arrive) var(--ease-instrument) both;
  }

  .tw-stage[data-active='true'] .tw-dl-contour {
    animation: tw-dl-settle 520ms var(--ease-entrance) 180ms both;
  }

  .tw-stage[data-active='true'] .tw-dl-flag {
    animation: tw-dl-acquire 300ms var(--ease-instrument) 440ms both;
  }

  /* quantum — the instrument assembles, outside in, and the state arrives last.
     860ms end to end: the silhouette traces itself, then the graticule, the
     axes and the latitude ring fade up in that order, then the labels and the
     readout, and only then the vector.

     Two rules govern this and both are load-bearing.

     1. **The animation may only touch `opacity` and `stroke-dashoffset`.**
        Nothing here writes a coordinate, so there is no moment where a
        keyframe and the scroll are both authoring the same attribute. That is
        what makes "no jump at activation" structural rather than tuned: the
        vector's geometry is already correct on frame zero, the arrival only
        reveals it.
     2. **No `transition` may ever be declared on `.tw-bl-shaft`'s `d`,
        `.tw-bl-head`'s `transform` or `.tw-bl-lat`'s `d`.** A transition there
        would smear the 1:1 scroll contract and fight reverse scroll, which is
        the whole point of the piece. `tests/bloch.test.ts` fails if one
        appears in this file.

     This world has no ambient loop, and that is the correct answer for it
     rather than an omission. Its motion is the reader's own scroll: θ tracks
     the traverse continuously, so through the whole centred dwell the state
     sweeps roughly 47° and the probability bars move with it. An idling
     animation on top of that would be a second, unrelated clock ticking over a
     value the reader is already driving. */
  .tw-stage[data-active='true'] .tw-bl-limb {
    animation: tw-draw 300ms var(--ease-instrument) both;
  }

  .tw-stage[data-active='true'] .tw-bl-veil,
  .tw-stage[data-active='true'] .tw-bl-arc,
  .tw-stage[data-active='true'] .tw-bl-axis,
  .tw-stage[data-active='true'] .tw-bl-lat,
  .tw-stage[data-active='true'] .tw-bl-labels,
  .tw-stage[data-active='true'] .tw-bl-readout,
  .tw-stage[data-active='true'] .tw-bl-vector {
    animation: tw-bl-in 300ms var(--ease-entrance) both;
  }

  .tw-stage[data-active='true'] .tw-bl-arc[data-arc='equator'] {
    animation-delay: 90ms;
  }
  .tw-stage[data-active='true'] .tw-bl-arc[data-arc='meridian'] {
    animation-delay: 150ms;
  }
  .tw-stage[data-active='true'] .tw-bl-axis {
    animation-delay: 210ms;
  }
  .tw-stage[data-active='true'] .tw-bl-lat {
    animation-delay: 260ms;
  }
  .tw-stage[data-active='true'] .tw-bl-labels,
  .tw-stage[data-active='true'] .tw-bl-readout {
    animation-delay: 420ms;
  }
  .tw-stage[data-active='true'] .tw-bl-vector {
    animation-delay: 560ms;
  }

  /* fpga — the selected route resolves, and its cells latch left to right.
     `:nth-child(8n + c)` is column `c - 1` of the 8-wide fabric, so the stagger
     sweeps in the direction the route is being drawn. */
  .tw-stage[data-active='true'] .tw-route[data-selected='true'] {
    animation: tw-draw var(--tw-arrive) var(--ease-instrument) both;
  }

  /* Two animations, and the order in the list is load-bearing: both write
     `fill`, and the ambient must win once it starts. `tw-latch` fires once on
     arrival, column by column; `tw-register` is the ambient that follows it —
     every claimed cell updating together on the clock edge, which is what
     synchronous logic actually does. Each `animation-delay` therefore carries
     two values, one per animation. */
  .tw-stage[data-active='true'] .tw-cell[data-claimed='true'] {
    animation:
      tw-latch 460ms var(--ease-instrument) both,
      tw-register 0.4s steps(2, jump-none) infinite;
  }
  .tw-stage[data-active='true'] .tw-cell[data-claimed='true']:nth-child(8n + 1) {
    animation-delay: 40ms, var(--tw-settle);
  }
  .tw-stage[data-active='true'] .tw-cell[data-claimed='true']:nth-child(8n + 2) {
    animation-delay: 110ms, var(--tw-settle);
  }
  .tw-stage[data-active='true'] .tw-cell[data-claimed='true']:nth-child(8n + 3) {
    animation-delay: 180ms, var(--tw-settle);
  }
  .tw-stage[data-active='true'] .tw-cell[data-claimed='true']:nth-child(8n + 4) {
    animation-delay: 250ms, var(--tw-settle);
  }
  .tw-stage[data-active='true'] .tw-cell[data-claimed='true']:nth-child(8n + 5) {
    animation-delay: 320ms, var(--tw-settle);
  }
  .tw-stage[data-active='true'] .tw-cell[data-claimed='true']:nth-child(8n + 6) {
    animation-delay: 390ms, var(--tw-settle);
  }
  .tw-stage[data-active='true'] .tw-cell[data-claimed='true']:nth-child(8n + 7) {
    animation-delay: 460ms, var(--tw-settle);
  }
  .tw-stage[data-active='true'] .tw-cell[data-claimed='true']:nth-child(8n + 8) {
    animation-delay: 530ms, var(--tw-settle);
  }

  /* electronics — the chain energises left to right, once. The overlay fades
     out at the end of its own keyframe, leaving the pointer-driven
     `.tw-trace-live` prefix as the resting picture. */
  .tw-stage[data-active='true'] .tw-trace-arrive {
    animation: tw-energise 1s var(--ease-instrument) both;
  }

  /* And the blocks come up behind it, in order. The trace alone is a hairline:
     what makes the arrival legible is the five stages of the chain lighting in
     the direction the signal travels. */
  .tw-stage[data-active='true'] .tw-blocks g[data-energised='true'] .tw-block {
    animation: tw-latch 520ms var(--ease-instrument) both;
  }
  .tw-stage[data-active='true'] .tw-blocks g:nth-child(1) .tw-block {
    animation-delay: 60ms;
  }
  .tw-stage[data-active='true'] .tw-blocks g:nth-child(2) .tw-block {
    animation-delay: 190ms;
  }
  .tw-stage[data-active='true'] .tw-blocks g:nth-child(3) .tw-block {
    animation-delay: 320ms;
  }
  .tw-stage[data-active='true'] .tw-blocks g:nth-child(4) .tw-block {
    animation-delay: 450ms;
  }
  .tw-stage[data-active='true'] .tw-blocks g:nth-child(5) .tw-block {
    animation-delay: 580ms;
  }

  /* audio — the waveform sweeps in and the spectrum rises to meet it.
     `fill-box` with a bottom origin makes the bars grow off the baseline they
     are already drawn on. */
  .tw-spectrum {
    transform-box: fill-box;
    transform-origin: 50% 100%;
  }

  .tw-stage[data-active='true'] .tw-wave {
    animation: tw-draw var(--tw-arrive) var(--ease-instrument) both;
  }

  .tw-stage[data-active='true'] .tw-spectrum {
    animation: tw-rise var(--tw-arrive) var(--ease-entrance) var(--tw-arrive-lag) both;
  }

  /* --- ai / decision landscape -------------------------------------------
     A three-class partition, the confidence corridor either side of it, the
     points it gets wrong, and a meter reading how much of the data it has
     accounted for. `--tw-progress` drives the boundary's shape, so this is the
     one stage on the band whose subject is literally the scroll.

     Two rules hold the colour scheme together. There is **exactly one accent
     object** — the boundary; and everything the reader is asked to read as
     *wrong* is `--color-on-ink`, never the accent, so error and partition are
     never competing for the same signal.

     Nothing here declares `opacity` on `.tw-dl-flag`, or `d` on any driven
     path. Those are written per frame as presentation attributes, and a CSS
     declaration outranks a presentation attribute — the writes would silently
     do nothing. See the header of `stages/ForecastStage.tsx`. ----------- */

  .tw-dl-grid line,
  .tw-dl-ticks line {
    stroke: var(--color-ink-rule);
    stroke-width: 1;
    opacity: 0.32;
    vector-effect: non-scaling-stroke;
  }

  /* The margin iso-contours: how far you can move before the model changes its
     mind. Ground, not subject — thin, muted, and the wider level fainter and
     dashed so the two read as one field sampled twice rather than as two
     outlines. */
  .tw-dl-contour {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--color-on-ink-muted);
    stroke-width: 1;
    opacity: 0.4;
    vector-effect: non-scaling-stroke;
  }

  .tw-dl-contour[data-level='outer'] {
    opacity: 0.24;
    stroke-dasharray: 2 3;
  }

  .tw-dl-glyph {
    vector-effect: non-scaling-stroke;
  }

  /* Solid, hollow, linear. Three marks that differ in kind, because at the true
     320px frame three radii of one circle do not differ at all — they collapse
     into one grey speck and the picture becomes dots either side of a curve. */
  .tw-dl-glyph[data-class='0'] {
    fill: var(--color-on-ink-muted);
  }

  .tw-dl-glyph[data-class='1'] {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--color-on-ink-muted);
    stroke-width: 1.2;
  }

  .tw-dl-glyph[data-class='2'] {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--color-on-ink-muted);
    stroke-width: 1.3;
    stroke-linecap: round;
  }

  /* A misclassified point, bracketed the way the stage frame brackets its own
     corners — which is the point: it is the same instrument marking something
     out. Deliberately not a ring, because class 1's glyph *is* a ring and a
     ring around a ring reads as a fourth kind of mark. */
  .tw-dl-flag {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--color-on-ink);
    stroke-width: 1.1;
    stroke-linecap: round;
    vector-effect: non-scaling-stroke;
    transform-box: fill-box;
    transform-origin: center;
  }

  /* The one accent object in the frame. */
  .tw-dl-boundary {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--tw-accent);
    stroke-width: 1.8;
    stroke-linecap: round;
    stroke-linejoin: round;
    vector-effect: non-scaling-stroke;
  }

  /* The ambient pass. Its dash geometry is declared here rather than only in
     the keyframe, so with `animation: none` it resolves to one short highlight
     on the partition instead of a solid white stroke laid over the accent. */
  .tw-dl-scan {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--color-on-ink);
    stroke-width: 2.2;
    stroke-linecap: round;
    stroke-dasharray: 11 100;
    opacity: 0.62;
    vector-effect: non-scaling-stroke;
  }

  .tw-dl-margin,
  .tw-dl-cross {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--color-on-ink);
    stroke-width: 1.1;
    vector-effect: non-scaling-stroke;
  }

  .tw-dl-margin {
    opacity: 0.75;
  }

  .tw-dl-query {
    fill: var(--color-on-ink);
  }

  /* The fit meter. Not `non-scaling-stroke`: it is a bar with a length, and a
     bar that stays 3px while its scale grows stops reading as one. */
  .tw-dl-fit-track {
    stroke: var(--color-ink-rule);
    stroke-width: 3;
    opacity: 0.35;
  }

  .tw-dl-fit-origin {
    stroke: var(--color-on-ink-muted);
    stroke-width: 1;
    vector-effect: non-scaling-stroke;
  }

  .tw-dl-fit {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--tw-accent);
    stroke-width: 3;
  }

  /* --- quantum / Bloch sphere ---------------------------------------------
     A state, not a diagram of one. `src/lib/visuals/bloch.ts` holds every
     coordinate; this block holds every colour, because the site's CSP forbids
     the inline `style` a canvas-based version would have needed to read a
     token back out of JavaScript.

     THE ONE RULE THAT IS NOT TASTE: `stroke-opacity` and `fill-opacity` must
     never appear below. Presentation attributes lose to CSS rules, so a single
     `stroke-opacity` declaration here would silently outrank the crossfade the
     component writes onto `.tw-bl-vector` and the arrow would stop passing
     behind the sphere. Only `fill`, `stroke`, `stroke-width`, `stroke-linecap`
     and dash geometry belong in this block. ------------------------------- */

  /* The graticule: equator, meridian, latitude ring. Hairlines, because the
     sphere is the frame of reference and not the subject. */
  .tw-bl-arc,
  .tw-bl-lat,
  .tw-bl-axis,
  .tw-bl-limb {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--color-ink-rule);
    stroke-width: 0.9;
    vector-effect: non-scaling-stroke;
  }

  .tw-bl-axis,
  .tw-bl-lat {
    stroke-width: 0.8;
  }

  /* Behind the sphere, dashed. Depth read as a line style rather than as a
     second colour, which is what a drafting convention would do and what keeps
     the accent reserved for the one thing that is actually the state. */
  .tw-bl-arc[data-face='back'],
  .tw-bl-lat[data-face='back'] {
    stroke-dasharray: 2.2 2.2;
  }

  .tw-bl-axis[data-face='back'] {
    stroke-dasharray: 1.6 2.4;
  }

  /* The glass — a wash the back half is seen *through*.

     Its 10% lives on the element as a `fill-opacity` attribute, NOT as an
     `opacity` declaration here, and the difference is not cosmetic: the arrival
     animates `opacity` from 0 to 1 with `both`, which would end by *replacing*
     a resting `opacity: 0.1` with 1 and leave an opaque grey disc over the
     whole sphere. It was drawn that way once. Animated opacity may only ever
     multiply a paint-level opacity, never stand in for one. */
  .tw-bl-veil {
    fill: var(--color-ink-rule);
    stroke: none;
  }

  /* The state vector. The only accented thing in the frame, in both copies. */
  .tw-bl-shaft {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--tw-accent);
    stroke-width: 1.75;
    stroke-linecap: round;
    vector-effect: non-scaling-stroke;
  }

  .tw-bl-head {
    fill: var(--tw-accent);
    stroke: none;
  }

  .tw-bl-label {
    fill: var(--color-on-ink-muted);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 9px;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
  }

  /* The label the state has actually reached. A basis state is a fact about
     the picture, so it is stated in the picture. */
  .tw-bl-label[data-hit='true'] {
    fill: var(--tw-accent);
  }

  .tw-bl-track {
    fill: var(--color-ink-rule);
    opacity: 0.22;
  }

  .tw-bl-bar {
    fill: var(--tw-accent);
  }

  /* ai ambient — one pass along the partition the model has settled on.

     The delay is negative on purpose: `--tw-settle` is 400ms against a 2.6s
     cycle, so `calc(var(--tw-settle) - 1.9s)` starts the pass 1.5s in. The
     world therefore arrives with a sweep already in flight rather than with a
     curve nothing has touched yet. Expressed against the same settle token
     every other stage is phased on, rather than as a bare negative constant,
     so retuning the settle moves this with it.

     It rides on an element the island renders only while the stage is active,
     so nothing here has a resting state that depends on an animation running
     (MOTION_SYSTEM §6). */
  .tw-stage[data-active='true'] .tw-dl-scan {
    animation: tw-dl-scan 2.6s linear calc(var(--tw-settle) - 1.9s) infinite;
  }

  /* --- fpga / routing ---------------------------------------------------- */

  .tw-channels line,
  .tw-clock line {
    stroke: var(--color-ink-rule);
    stroke-width: 1;
    opacity: 0.4;
    vector-effect: non-scaling-stroke;
  }

  .tw-clock-active {
    fill: var(--tw-accent);
  }

  .tw-cell {
    fill: var(--color-ink-raised);
    stroke: var(--color-ink-rule);
    stroke-width: 1;
    vector-effect: non-scaling-stroke;
    transition: stroke var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-standard);
  }

  .tw-cell[data-claimed='true'] {
    stroke: var(--tw-accent);
  }

  .tw-route {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--color-ink-rule);
    stroke-width: 1.2;
    opacity: 0.5;
    vector-effect: non-scaling-stroke;
    transition:
      stroke var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-standard),
      opacity var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-standard);
  }

  .tw-route[data-selected='true'] {
    stroke: var(--tw-accent);
    stroke-width: 1.6;
    opacity: 1;
  }

  /* --- electronics / signal path ----------------------------------------- */

  .tw-trace {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--color-ink-rule);
    stroke-width: 1.4;
    vector-effect: non-scaling-stroke;
  }

  /* `stroke-dasharray` arrives as a presentation attribute in a `pathLength`
     normalised 0–100 space, so it cannot be transitioned — attributes are not
     animatable properties. That is the deliberate trade for a CSP-safe island,
     and an instant response to a pointer is the more instrument-like reading
     anyway. */
  .tw-trace-live {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--tw-accent);
    stroke-width: 1.6;
    vector-effect: non-scaling-stroke;
  }

  .tw-vias circle {
    fill: var(--color-ink);
    stroke: var(--color-ink-rule);
    stroke-width: 1;
    vector-effect: non-scaling-stroke;
  }

  .tw-block {
    fill: var(--color-ink-raised);
    stroke: var(--color-ink-rule);
    stroke-width: 1;
    vector-effect: non-scaling-stroke;
    transition: stroke var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-standard);
  }

  .tw-block-glyph {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--color-on-ink-muted);
    stroke-width: 1.3;
    stroke-linecap: round;
    stroke-linejoin: round;
    vector-effect: non-scaling-stroke;
    transition: stroke var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-standard);
  }

  .tw-blocks g[data-energised='true'] .tw-block {
    stroke: var(--tw-accent);
  }

  .tw-blocks g[data-energised='true'] .tw-block-glyph {
    stroke: var(--color-on-ink);
  }

  .tw-probe {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--tw-accent);
    stroke-width: 1;
    vector-effect: non-scaling-stroke;
  }

  /* --- audio / waveform -------------------------------------------------- */

  .tw-graticule line {
    stroke: var(--color-ink-rule);
    stroke-width: 1;
    opacity: 0.4;
    vector-effect: non-scaling-stroke;
  }

  .tw-wave {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--tw-accent);
    stroke-width: 1.6;
    stroke-linejoin: round;
    vector-effect: non-scaling-stroke;
  }

  /* Declared explicitly so `translateX(-100%)` in the travel keyframe resolves
     against the view box (one frame width) rather than this group's own
     bounding box, which is two tiles wide. */
  .tw-wave-travel {
    transform-box: view-box;
    transform-origin: 0 0;
  }

  .tw-spectrum rect {
    fill: var(--color-on-ink-muted);
    opacity: 0.5;
  }

  .tw-spectrum rect[data-peak='true'] {
    fill: var(--tw-accent);
    opacity: 1;
  }

  /* Audio ambient is the travelling wave alone. The per-bar opacity breathe
     that used to sit on top of it was decoration: the spectrum's meaning is
     its *shape*, which the pointer already changes, and blinking the bars said
     nothing the wave was not already saying (REDESIGN_DECISIONS P2, Audio). */
  /* 3.6s, and starting a third of the way in.

     At 7.5s behind an 800ms settle this was the worst offender on the band: a
     world's whole dwell could pass before the wave had crossed a fifth of a
     tile, so the one thing this stage says — that a waveform *travels* — was
     never actually shown. The negative delay is the same device the FPGA
     fabric's register blink already uses successfully: the wave is already moving
     the instant the world arrives, rather than starting from a standstill at
     keyframe 0 with nothing having happened yet. There is no phase to protect
     here — the second tile makes the loop seamless from any offset. */
  .tw-stage[data-active='true'] .tw-wave-travel {
    animation: tw-wave-travel 3.6s linear infinite;
    animation-delay: -1.2s;
  }

  /* --- Signal pulses -----------------------------------------------------
     Rendered by the island only while a stage is active, so there is no
     resting element with `opacity: 0` anywhere in this section. --------- */

  .tw-route-pulse,
  .tw-trace-arrive,
  .tw-trace-pulse {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--tw-accent);
    stroke-linecap: round;
    vector-effect: non-scaling-stroke;
  }

  /* FPGA: **one cell advanced per clock tick.** `steps(6)` over a
     `pathLength`-100 route is six discrete advances per traversal, matching
     `ROUTE_STEPS` in `stages/RoutingStage.tsx` — asserted equal by
     `tests/worlds.test.ts`, since a timing function cannot read a module
     constant. The linear offset this replaced read as *flow*, which is the
     wrong physics: a fabric latches on an edge, it does not trickle. */
  /* The negative delay is exactly two clock ticks, so the pulse is already two
     cells along when the world arrives and is still on the same 0.4s edge as
     `.tw-clock-active` below. A pulse that starts from cell zero every time a
     world is entered reads as the fabric booting; a fabric is always clocking. */
  .tw-route-pulse {
    stroke-width: 3.6;
    stroke-dasharray: 17 83;
    animation: tw-pulse-travel 2.4s steps(6, end) infinite;
    animation-delay: -0.8s;
  }

  /* The clock those ticks are counted against: 2.4s / 6 = one blink per
     advance. `steps(2, jump-none)` holds each half of the period, which is a
     square wave rather than a fade. */
  .tw-stage[data-active='true'] .tw-clock-active {
    animation: tw-clock-tick 0.4s steps(2, jump-none) infinite;
  }

  /* Electronics stays smooth, because that domain genuinely is continuous. */
  .tw-trace-pulse {
    stroke-width: 3;
    stroke-dasharray: 22 78;
    animation: tw-pulse-travel var(--tw-chain) linear infinite;
    animation-delay: var(--tw-chain-phase);
  }

  .tw-trace-arrive {
    stroke-width: 2.6;
  }

  /* And the chain carries state: each block's glyph brightens in turn, in
     order, so the signal is visibly passing through the stages rather than the
     trace merely glowing.

     Its period is the pulse's own `--tw-chain` and its stagger is a fifth of
     that (0.48s), so a block brightens as the pulse reaches it. Same idea as
     the quantum fringe: the response has to be caused by the thing arriving, or
     the two halves of the picture are just sharing a box. Both are offset by
     `--tw-chain-phase`, which is what keeps them in step now that the pulse
     starts mid-flight rather than after a settle. */
  .tw-stage[data-active='true'] .tw-blocks g[data-energised='true'] .tw-block-glyph {
    animation: tw-block-step var(--tw-chain) var(--ease-instrument) infinite;
  }
  .tw-stage[data-active='true'] .tw-blocks g:nth-child(1) .tw-block-glyph {
    animation-delay: calc(var(--tw-chain-phase) + 0.12s);
  }
  .tw-stage[data-active='true'] .tw-blocks g:nth-child(2) .tw-block-glyph {
    animation-delay: calc(var(--tw-chain-phase) + 0.6s);
  }
  .tw-stage[data-active='true'] .tw-blocks g:nth-child(3) .tw-block-glyph {
    animation-delay: calc(var(--tw-chain-phase) + 1.08s);
  }
  .tw-stage[data-active='true'] .tw-blocks g:nth-child(4) .tw-block-glyph {
    animation-delay: calc(var(--tw-chain-phase) + 1.56s);
  }
  .tw-stage[data-active='true'] .tw-blocks g:nth-child(5) .tw-block-glyph {
    animation-delay: calc(var(--tw-chain-phase) + 2.04s);
  }

  /* ===========================================================================
     KEYFRAMES
     Every one of these perturbs a state that is already visible. None of them
     is the reason anything is on screen.
     ======================================================================== */

  /* ── Arrival ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Each of these is the *hidden* half of an element whose resting state is
     already the finished picture. Read every `from` as "what this looked like
     for the instant before the world arrived", never as "how this element is
     defined". With `animation: none`, none of them applies. ------------- */

  /* A line drawing itself along its own length. Both stops carry the dash
     geometry so the finished state is a solid stroke, not a dashed one. */
  @keyframes tw-draw {
    from {
      stroke-dasharray: 100 100;
      stroke-dashoffset: 100;
    }
    to {
      stroke-dasharray: 100 100;
      stroke-dashoffset: 0;
    }
  }

  /* ai: the interval opens rightward out of the `now` rule. The `to` stop is
     an outset inset — a clip larger than the box — so the finished state clips
     nothing at all and no stroke is shaved at the edges. */
  @keyframes tw-fan-open {
    from {
      clip-path: inset(-30% 100% -30% -2%);
      opacity: 0.2;
    }
    to {
      clip-path: inset(-30% -30% -30% -2%);
      opacity: 1;
    }
  }

  /* quantum: one element of the instrument coming up. Opacity only — the Bloch
     stage's geometry belongs to the scroll from frame zero, and a keyframe that
     also wrote a coordinate would be a second author of the same attribute. */
  @keyframes tw-bl-in {
    from {
      opacity: 0;
    }
    to {
      opacity: 1;
    }
  }

  /* fpga: a cell claimed by the resolving route. Ends where it started, which
     is also the cell's resting fill — the latch is the event, not the state. */
  @keyframes tw-latch {
    0% {
      fill: var(--color-ink-raised);
    }
    35% {
      fill: var(--tw-accent);
    }
    100% {
      fill: var(--color-ink-raised);
    }
  }

  /* electronics: the chain energises end to end, then hands the frame back to
     the pointer-driven prefix underneath it. */
  @keyframes tw-energise {
    0% {
      stroke-dasharray: 100 100;
      stroke-dashoffset: 100;
      opacity: 1;
    }
    72% {
      stroke-dasharray: 100 100;
      stroke-dashoffset: 0;
      opacity: 1;
    }
    100% {
      stroke-dasharray: 100 100;
      stroke-dashoffset: 0;
      opacity: 0;
    }
  }

  /* audio: the spectrum rises off the baseline to meet the swept-in wave. */
  @keyframes tw-rise {
    from {
      transform: scaleY(0.05);
    }
    to {
      transform: scaleY(1);
    }
  }

  /* ── Ambient ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */

  /* ai: a pass travelling the length of the decision boundary. Written in the
     normalised `pathLength="100"` space, so one set of offsets serves a
     partition whose real length changes on every scroll tick. */
  @keyframes tw-dl-scan {
    from {
      stroke-dashoffset: 11;
    }
    to {
      stroke-dashoffset: -100;
    }
  }

  /* ai: the confidence corridor settling either side of the resolved boundary.

     Deliberately a `from` with no `to`. The implicit 100% keyframe is the
     element's own underlying value, so each contour lands on the opacity its
     own rule gives it — 0.3 for the inner level, 0.16 for the outer — without
     this keyframe having to restate either number, or having to exist twice. */
  @keyframes tw-dl-settle {
    from {
      opacity: 0;
      stroke-width: 3;
    }
  }

  /* ai: a flag closing onto the point it marks. Transform only: the flags'
     opacity is a per-frame presentation attribute and must stay writable. */
  @keyframes tw-dl-acquire {
    from {
      transform: scale(2.4);
    }
    to {
      transform: scale(1);
    }
  }

  /* fpga: the clock edge the pulse advances on. */
  @keyframes tw-clock-tick {
    from {
      opacity: 1;
    }
    to {
      opacity: 0.25;
    }
  }

  /* fpga: every cell the net claims updating on that same edge. A pipeline
     does not trickle — on the edge, all of it moves at once. The tint is a
     `color-mix` toward the accent rather than the accent itself, because a
     register update is a state change, not an alarm. */
  @keyframes tw-register {
    from {
      fill: color-mix(in oklab, var(--tw-accent) 26%, var(--color-ink-raised));
    }
    to {
      fill: var(--color-ink-raised);
    }
  }

  /* electronics: the signal passing through one block of the chain. */
  @keyframes tw-block-step {
    0%,
    100% {
      opacity: 0.5;
    }
    10% {
      opacity: 1;
    }
    32% {
      opacity: 0.5;
    }
  }

  /* One tile is one frame width, and `transform-box: view-box` makes 100% mean
     exactly that — so the shift is expressed without handing CSS a measured
     length through an inline custom property. The second copy of the wave is
     drawn one tile to the right, so the loop has no seam. */
  @keyframes tw-wave-travel {
    from {
      transform: translateX(0);
    }
    to {
      transform: translateX(-100%);
    }
  }

  /* The pulsed paths declare `pathLength="100"`, so their dash geometry lives
     in a normalised 0–100 space and one offset serves every route regardless of
     its real length. */
  @keyframes tw-pulse-travel {
    from {
      stroke-dashoffset: 0;
    }
    to {
      stroke-dashoffset: -100;
    }
  }

  /* ===========================================================================
     REDUCED MOTION — MOTION_SYSTEM §6

     The real gate is in JavaScript: the island's `matchMedia` check means the
     GSAP timeline and the ScrollTrigger are never constructed, so there is no
     pin, no horizontal translation and no scroll distance to reclaim. The
     section stays the vertical stack it was server-rendered as, and every
     stage renders one static representative frame.

     This block is the second belt. It removes the ambient stage animation at
     the CSS level as well, and it can do so with `animation: none` precisely
     because no element here depends on an animation to become visible.
     ======================================================================== */

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .tw .tw-stage *,
    .tw .tw-stage {
      animation: none !important;
    }

    .tw-panel,
    .tw-rail__stop,
    .tw-stage__channel,
    .tw-stage__frame .tw-tick,
    .tw-cell,
    .tw-route,
    .tw-trace-live,
    .tw-block,
    .tw-block-glyph {
      transition: none !important;
    }
  }
</style>
